1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
In the second unit we are going to discuss the geometric image formation process and basic camera models.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:14,000
We're going to see how points in 3D space are related to points on the 2D image plane.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:25,000
The principle of projection is an old one and as an example we can take for instance the human eye where

4
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:34,000
light is passing through a lens and an aperture and is then collected by photoreceptors on the retina.

5
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:43,000
And the same principle holds true for the first camera obscura which is collecting light through a little tiny hole

6
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:51,000
that's made into the wall of an otherwise entirely dark room and that gives rise to an image on the upper wall

7
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:58,000
and then all the way until the photographic camera has come to life.

8
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:09,000
So here's an example of the camera obscura where an entirely dark room is illuminated by the light that is passing through this tiny little hole

9
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:22,000
and creating an image of the objects outside of the scene outside upside down in this very dark room.

10
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Of course because the aperture is so small and there's so little light coming into the room you have to adapt to the darkness of that room

11
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,000
if you're standing inside that room to actually perceive something.

12
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,000
It's actually just very little light.

13
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,000
It's not very bright if you create such a camera obscura at home.

14
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,000
But this has been used.

15
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:49,000
You can find this in various museums.

16
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:57,000
It has been used by artists here some examples and on this website you find more examples where ordinary rooms have been illuminated

17
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:07,000
using this principle of a very small pinhole and then an image has been captured inside that room with a very long shutter time

18
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:17,000
and giving rise to installations like you can see here by this artist.

19
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:30,000
So the most basic physical pinhole camera can be described as a box that has a little hole inside one of the walls

20
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:44,000
and that hole is collecting light or is letting light pass through but it's letting light pass through only into the direction continuing where the light is coming from.

21
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:57,000
So we have a point here then that light passes through that hole and because light travels linearly along rays that light is hitting this image plane

22
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:04,000
the sensor here on the back exactly at one location.

23
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:14,000
And because of this the image that is projected is actually projected upside down onto that image plane behind this little hole in the wall

24
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:25,000
which is called a focal point because all the light rays that pass through this hole must pass exactly through this hole this focal point.

25
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:36,000
However when we consider camera models in this lecture we typically consider the image plane to be in front of the focal point and not behind the focal point.

26
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:45,000
So here is an example of despite this being the physical model the mathematical model looks like this here on the right.

27
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:55,000
The distance from the focal point of this from the focal point of this plane is the same as the distance of this image plane to this focal point

28
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,000
but in this case the image plane is in front of the focal point.

29
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Now in this case the image of the 3D object here is of the same size and the same shape as the image if it's projected behind the focal point

30
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:25,000
the only difference is that it's mirrored and so both models are actually equivalent right they are just related by this mirroring

31
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:32,000
so they can be brought into each other using appropriate change of image coordinates.

32
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:43,000
Now in this lecture we're considering this mathematical camera model which has some advantages in the sense that the image is not projected upside down

33
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:51,000
so we can directly see the image as we would see it as humans and that's also how digital cameras store images.

34
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:57,000
They don't store the images upside down but they store them in the right orientation.

35
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:08,000
So this is what we can use as a replacement for this model because both are equivalent up to very simple reflection.

36
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Now if we talk about projection models there's two basic projection models that are important.

37
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,000
There's many more but these two are the ones that we're going to discuss here

38
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:29,000
and if you want to know about our projection models I recommend having a look into the Sileski book in chapter 214.

39
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:44,000
The first model that we're going to discuss here is the so-called orthographic projection model where light is assumed to travel parallel to each other's ray

40
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:51,000
and then projected in that parallel fashion onto that image plane

41
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:57,000
and in the perspective projection model which is the pinhole camera model where we have a focal point

42
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:03,000
and all the light rays that pass from any 3D point to that focal point

43
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:13,000
all the light rays must pass through that focal point are hitting the image plane at the intersection of that line with the image plane.

44
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:21,000
You can see that here in this case the size of the object changes while here the size remains the same.

45
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:26,000
The object has the same 3D size as the dimensions on the image plane

46
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:31,000
and both of these projection models are relevant.

47
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:37,000
The traditional projection model is really the perspective projection model that is implemented

48
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:45,000
that can approximate almost all the cameras that we know like our smartphones, our digital cameras or DSLRs

49
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:52,000
but if we use a very large focal length, if we have a telephoto lens

50
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:59,000
then this perspective projection model can be well approximated by an orthographic projection model.

51
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:03,000
The perspective effects vanish if we use a telephoto lens

52
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:12,000
and in other cases that is actually wanted for instance in applications where we want to use optics for measurement.

53
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:21,000
Here on the left you can see a so called telecentric lens that models exactly the orthographic projection process

54
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:29,000
because if you model that process exactly and you can measure distances then afterwards after projection on the image plane

55
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:34,000
you know that these distances correspond one to one to the distances in the real world.

56
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:39,000
So this is used for inspection purposes for instance.

57
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:44,000
Here is a comparison between perspective and orthographic projection

58
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:51,000
and what they do to the image that is formed and that there is a continuous transition in between these two

59
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:56,000
and that's also what you're going to play with and experiment with in the exercise.

60
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:03,000
So here on the left we have a classic, this is all the same cube but here it's projected using a perspective projection

61
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:11,000
where the vanishing point is in the vicinity and not at infinity

62
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:18,000
and we can see that these edges of that 3D cube that are all parallel to each other in 3D

63
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:24,000
are intersecting at this vanishing point that we see here on this image.

64
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:31,000
Now if we increase the focal length we come to a weak perspective setting

65
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:40,000
or closer to a weak perspective setting where this vanishing point moves outside this image domain that we're considering here

66
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:47,000
and if we increase the focal length even further if we move the camera infinitely far away from the object

67
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:51,000
and at the same time increase the focal length to infinity

68
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:56,000
which of course is physically impossible without such a telecentric lens

69
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:03,000
then we obtain an orthographic projection here where then all the lines that are parallel in 3D

70
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:11,000
are actually also parallel in the image that is projected in the image that we have captured with the camera

71
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:15,000
and here at the bottom you can see two examples of a perspective projection

72
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:22,000
and a orthographic projection or close to orthographic projection of a real scene.

73
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:28,000
So how can we describe these projections mathematically?

74
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:32,000
Here we see the model for the orthographic projection

75
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:39,000
and in all illustrations that I'm showing here the illustrations are only showing the X and the C coordinate

76
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:47,000
the Y coordinate you can think of as going into the image plane here of this camera coordinate system in red

77
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:57,000
but it's just difficult to draw in 3D and it's confusing so I draw everything just as this cut in X and C dimensions.

78
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:02,000
So what we have here is the camera center, the origin where the camera coordinate system is defined

79
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:10,000
this is the XYC coordinate system where the 3D coordinates of a 3D point and 3D space are defined

80
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:17,000
and then in the orthographic projection model the image coordinate system falls together with the X and the Y axis

81
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:24,000
here only X illustrated again so we have the X axis of the image coordinate system in blue

82
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:31,000
and the X axis of the camera coordinate system in red that are the same and the same holds true for the Y axis.

83
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:41,000
And now because the light rays travel parallel to the so-called principle line here and parallel to each other

84
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:50,000
we know that if we have this point X, C stands for camera coordinates and S stands for screen coordinates or image coordinates

85
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:56,000
we know that the X and the Y coordinates are actually remaining the same.

86
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:07,000
So everything that we have done in this projection is we have removed the C coordinate but X, S, so this is the 3D point

87
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:14,000
and the non-bold version is the X coordinate of that 3D point

88
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:22,000
and that X coordinate of the 3D point is projected to the same value in the image plane so the X coordinate in screen coordinates

89
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:26,000
is the same value as the X coordinate in the camera coordinates

90
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:31,000
and the same holds true for the Y coordinate.

91
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:44,000
So this is how another graphic projection of a 3D point bold X, C and 3D space to pixel coordinates bold X, S in the 2D image domain here works.

92
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:49,000
The Y and the X axis of the camera and the image coordinate systems are shared

93
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:54,000
the light rays are parallel to the C coordinate of the camera coordinate system

94
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:58,000
and during project it is also called the principal axis here in this black one

95
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:05,000
and during projection the C coordinate is dropped and X and Y remain the same.

96
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:11,000
And just to highlight it again the Y coordinate is not shown here for clarity

97
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:18,000
but you can think of the Y coordinate as going into the direction of the image plane, the direction we can see here

98
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:21,000
but it behaves exactly the same.

99
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:25,000
So how can we write this down in terms of linear algebra?

100
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:31,000
Well we know that an autographic projection simply drops the C component of the 3D point

101
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:34,000
but leaves the other coordinates the same

102
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:38,000
and so we can simply express this in inhomogeneous coordinates as such

103
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:47,000
where we have a 3D point multiplied with this 2 by 3 matrix where the last coordinate of this 3D point is multiplied with 0

104
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:53,000
and the first and the second coordinate are copied.

105
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:59,000
And we have an equivalent representation in homogeneous coordinates here with these augmented vectors

106
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:03,000
in camera coordinates in 3D space so this is a 4D vector

107
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:08,000
and here in screen coordinates now this is a three-dimensional vector

108
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:14,000
where we take this four-dimensional vector and the last element is a 1 because it's an augmented vector

109
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:18,000
so the last element of XS will also be a 1 in this case

110
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:22,000
but of course again this can be replaced by any homogeneous coordinates

111
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:25,000
it doesn't need to be augmented vectors.

112
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:29,000
Autography as already mentioned is exact for telecentric lenses

113
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:32,000
and an approximation for telephoto lenses

114
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:37,000
and after projection the distance of the 3D point from the image can't be recovered anymore

115
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:42,000
which is a property that holds true for whole projections also for perspective projection of course.

116
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:48,000
So after the projection onto the image plane the distance is unknown.

117
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:57,000
Now in practice we're not going to use exactly that model that I've just shown

118
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:03,000
because the image sensor typically measures in pixels

119
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:09,000
we define the image coordinate system in pixels not in millimeters or meters

120
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:16,000
and so we must scale the coordinates in order to be able to measure in pixels

121
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,000
and that's called scaled autography

122
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:22,000
and so what we have here is simply replace these ones with an S scaling factor

123
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:28,000
so we can scale the X and the Y coordinate that we're projecting

124
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:31,000
and still dropping the C coordinate of course

125
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:37,000
and the unit of this scaling factor is pixel over meter or pixel over millimeter

126
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:44,000
depending on the unit of the 3D point in order to be able to convert the metric 3D points into pixels.

127
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:48,000
So let's assume we have 10 pixels per millimeter

128
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:52,000
and we have a point that is at X 10 millimeters

129
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:59,000
then we would convert this into 100 pixels.

130
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:06,000
Under autography structure and motion can be estimated simultaneously using closed form factorization methods

131
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:13,000
and we're going to see some of these methods in the next lecture.

132
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:17,000
Now having seen the simple autographic projection model

133
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:24,000
we come to the perspective projection model which is a model that we need for almost all traditional cameras

134
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:31,000
where light rays are focused at a single focal point which is here the camera center.

135
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:40,000
So here's an example of how a 3D point XC in camera coordinates projects to pixel coordinates XS in

136
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:45,000
so here's the image plane now in the screen coordinates.

137
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:53,000
As you can see now the image plane and image coordinate system are not co-located with the camera coordinate system anymore as before

138
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:59,000
but the image plane is displaced from out of the camera center

139
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:12,000
and all the rays here must pass through this focal point, through this camera center.

140
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:20,000
So there's always the light ray that passes through the camera center, the pixel XS and the 3D point XC.

141
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
This is the constraint.

142
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:30,000
And the convention here is that the principal axis is, which is this one here, is orthogonal to the image plane

143
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:33,000
and aligns with the C axis.

144
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:38,000
So it's called the principal axis.

145
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:44,000
And again the Y coordinate is not shown here for clarity but it behaves similarly.

146
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:50,000
So how does a 3D point now project mathematically to this point XS?

147
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:59,000
Well, for this 3D point we know the X coordinate and we know the C coordinate in 3D camera coordinates

148
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:10,000
and for this image coordinate we know the X coordinate on screen in pixels let's say and we know the focal length F.

149
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:15,000
This is the focal length that is the distance of the image plane from the focal point.

150
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:20,000
That's a parameter of cameras. We can change it. If you have a zoom lens we can change the focal length.

151
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:26,000
That will change how big the object will appear on the image plane.

152
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:34,000
You can already imagine changing this will change the size of the projection onto the image

153
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:39,000
because the image plane moves, it translates left to right.

154
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:44,000
Now what is the relationship that we can infer from this geometric constellation?

155
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:48,000
Well, we see that there is a set of equal triangles.

156
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:53,000
So we have this triangle here XS and F.

157
00:17:53,000 --> 00:18:08,000
So if we divide XS divided by F we must have the same ratio as if we divide XC by CC just by the principle of equal triangles.

158
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:14,000
So we have this triangle, this big triangle here and this little small triangle here.

159
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:17,000
And this is what I've written here on the right hand side.

160
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:29,000
So in other words I can solve this for XS by saying well the pixel coordinate X is equal to XC divided by CC multiplied with the focal length F.

161
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:33,000
And this is exactly the pinhole projection formula.

162
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:41,000
And it also holds for Y of course, YS is equal to YC divided by CC times F.

163
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:52,000
Now we can also formulate this mathematically in terms of a matrix multiplication if we use homogeneous coordinates.

164
00:18:52,000 --> 00:19:00,000
So as we've seen in perspective projection 3D points and camera coordinates are mapped to the image plane by dividing them by their C component

165
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:04,000
and multiplying with the focal length. That's what we've just derived.

166
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:13,000
And this is this expression here for both the X and the Y coordinates in regular in homogeneous coordinates.

167
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:23,000
Now while this is a nonlinear expression because we have to divide by C in homogeneous coordinates we can write this as a linear equation.

168
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:32,000
So assume a matrix called a camera matrix or projection matrix.

169
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:40,000
There's a 3 by 4 matrix here with the focal length on the diagonal and zeros in the last column.

170
00:19:40,000 --> 00:20:06,000
And if we multiply this with the homogeneous four-dimensional augmented vector XC of the 3D point then we obtain well XCF, YCF and then the third coordinate CC.

171
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:16,000
And if we now know this is a homogeneous three-dimensional homogeneous vector and if we normalize that homogeneous vector into an augmented vector where the last element is one,

172
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:26,000
we obtain exactly FXC over CC and FYC over CC in the first two coordinates elements of this vector.

173
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:42,000
So by using this homogeneous representation because we have these points that are all equivalent to each other and by the definition of conversion from homogeneous coordinates to inhomogeneous coordinates,

174
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:59,000
we are representing the projection process so that we can express this entire process here with a simple linear multiplication if we assume homogeneous coordinates and then afterwards do the conversion to inhomogeneous coordinates

175
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:04,000
by dividing by the third element of the homogeneous vector.

176
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:09,000
So the important point here is this projection is linear when using homogeneous coordinates.

177
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:17,000
And after the projection again as in the autographic case it's impossible to recover the distance of the 3D point from the image.

178
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:26,000
As a little remark the unit for F is typically chosen as pixels to convert metric 3D points into pixels.

179
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:37,000
If you look at this expression here, if we have a 3D point that's expressed in meters, so we have X and C being defined as meters,

180
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:52,000
but the screen coordinates are supposed to be in pixels and of course the unit of F has to be also defined in pixels because the meters cancel here and so the only thing that remains is the screen coordinate in pixel.

181
00:21:52,000 --> 00:22:07,000
And that's why the focal length in our models that we're using is often going to be defined in pixels.

182
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:19,000
This is the most basic form of the pinhole projection principle, but we can add more parameters and one important parameter is the so-called principle point.

183
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:31,000
Why do we need a principle point? Well, in the previous model that I've explained the image coordinate system and this is now a 3D illustration of this one here.

184
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:45,000
The image coordinate system is defined on the principle axis, is centered on the principle axis, but it's inconvenient to store pixels with negative coordinates.

185
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:50,000
Consider a pixel here that would have negative coordinates with respect to that coordinate system.

186
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:59,000
So what is done is in practice and also I have already as you can see here oriented the coordinate system in a way that is convenient to store images.

187
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:05,000
What we can do here is we can move that coordinate system to the top left of the image.

188
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:14,000
So this is the view point here. So looking from behind, this is the top left corner of the image.

189
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:23,000
And so the first pixel here, well, if we move this coordinate system will be located at 0, 0 or 1, 1 if you use a one based coordinate system,

190
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:28,000
which is much more convenient to store image arrays. And that's what we typically do in practice.

191
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:35,000
We don't consider negative pixels, but we consider just positive pixels and define the image coordinate system here,

192
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:47,000
which means that this principle point here must be added to the image coordinates such that we obtain only positive coordinates.

193
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:59,000
And that's called the principle point CX and CY that's added to the pixel coordinates after this projection that was discussed in the previous slide.

194
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:10,000
So the complete perspective projection model without distortion is given by this equation here,

195
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:18,000
where you can see that now in addition to this multiplying the focal length with X divided by C,

196
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:24,000
we have also now a translation by the principle point.

197
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:27,000
And we have done two more modifications here.

198
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:34,000
So one modification is that we have allowed the focal length in X direction to differ from the focal length in Y direction.

199
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:47,000
And we have added another scaling factor here that allows for, this is called this Q,

200
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:57,000
that allows for modeling sensors that are not mounted perpendicular to the optical axis due to manufacturing inaccuracies.

201
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:08,000
FX and FY however would only be independent from each other if we have different pixel aspect ratios and sensors are manufactured typically pretty precise.

202
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:18,000
So in practice what we often do is we set FX equal to FY and also this Q is typically very small so that in practice often this Q is set to zero.

203
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:23,000
But we have to model in almost all cases that we consider the principle point.

204
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:34,000
So while F can be set the same and S can be set to zero, the principle point is still an important parameter that we want to estimate that we want to model.

205
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:41,000
Now this is the full camera matrix that is defined through all of these parameters and this is called the intrinsic matrix.

206
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:54,000
And actually this is the projection matrix, the 3 by 4 matrix, but the 3 by 3 sub matrix here in front is called the calibration matrix K here in red.

207
00:25:54,000 --> 00:26:00,000
And this is called the intrinsic matrix because it stores all the intrinsic parameters of the camera.

208
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:09,000
Everything that's intrinsic to the camera like the focal length, the sensor ratio, the pixel ratio, the principle point and so on.

209
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:17,000
And this is opposed to the so-called extrinsic parameters which is the camera pose with respect to some world coordinate system.

210
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:20,000
That's why it's called intrinsic parameters.

211
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:28,000
And the projection then can be done in homogeneous coordinates by just taking this four-dimensional homogeneous vector,

212
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:35,000
representing a 3D point in camera coordinate system and projecting it by multiplying it with this matrix.

213
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:41,000
Now one thing that's nice about this homogeneous representation is that we can change transformations.

214
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:49,000
So if a 3D point is not represented in camera coordinate systems but is represented in the world coordinate system,

215
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:59,000
we can change the transformation, the rigid body transformation that maps a point by multiplying it with this rotation and translation matrix.

216
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:04,000
This is an Euclidean transformation that we've seen before.

217
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:11,000
So it multiplies that point and afterwards multiplies that point that is now in camera coordinates with the intrinsic matrix.

218
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:15,000
So we have the extrinsic and the intrinsic matrix here.

219
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:23,000
On to the image plane, we can also do this by simply multiplying these two transformations together into one matrix.

220
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:28,000
And now we have just to do one matrix multiplication instead of two matrix multiplications.

221
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:34,000
And in many cases that's very convenient and can also save time and be more efficient.

222
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:39,000
So here's the mathematical formulation of this.

223
00:27:39,000 --> 00:27:48,000
So we have the screen coordinate which is equal to the camera coordinate or camera, the 3D point in camera coordinates,

224
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:56,000
multiplied with the intrinsic which is equal to the point in world coordinate,

225
00:27:56,000 --> 00:28:03,000
multiplied with the extrinsic and the intrinsic and then we can multiply these two matrices together.

226
00:28:03,000 --> 00:28:07,000
This is a 3 by 3 matrix and this is a 3 by 4 matrix.

227
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:16,000
So obtain a 3 by 4 projection matrix that directly maps a point from world coordinates to the screen.

228
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:26,000
And this 3 by 4 projection matrix P can be pre-computed, can be pre-multiplied and then applied for all the points where we want to apply this projection on.

229
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:39,000
Sometimes it's preferable to use a full-rank 4 by 4 projection matrix where instead of 3 by 4 matrix with the intrinsic

230
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:51,000
and a 4 by 4 matrix for the extrinsics we have also a 4 by 4 matrix for the intrinsic

231
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:57,000
where we simply have added the last row of zeros and a one in the last element.

232
00:28:57,000 --> 00:29:02,000
Now this is of course if we multiply these together we get a 4 by 4 matrix

233
00:29:02,000 --> 00:29:11,000
and now we can take this homogeneous vector, this 4D vector representing a 3D point

234
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:18,000
and project it through this matrix and obtain now a four-dimensional homogeneous vector x tilde

235
00:29:18,000 --> 00:29:27,000
but because we know this is a point that's on the image screen we must still normalize this vector with respect to the third coordinate,

236
00:29:27,000 --> 00:29:29,000
not with respect to the fourth coordinate.

237
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:35,000
And so what we obtain is this expression here which is familiar because the first three coordinates are the same,

238
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:44,000
the third coordinate is one by definition and we have this projection here in the first two coordinates

239
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:55,000
and then the last coordinate here is the inverse step as you can see here.

240
00:29:55,000 --> 00:30:02,000
And so because this fourth component of this inhomogeneous 4D vector is the inverse step,

241
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:08,000
if the inverse step is known we can use this full-rank matrix to also do the inverse.

242
00:30:08,000 --> 00:30:14,000
So assume we have this vector where we know the inverse step as well,

243
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:24,000
then we can also now because it's a full-rank matrix we can invert it and go from an image point to the 3D point

244
00:30:24,000 --> 00:30:29,000
and afterwards in order to extract the 3D point we need to normalize with respect to the fourth entry

245
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:32,000
because now we have a point in 3D space.

246
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:41,000
But of course we need to know the depth otherwise this wouldn't be possible.

247
00:30:41,000 --> 00:30:45,000
Good.

248
00:30:45,000 --> 00:30:53,000
Now the last aspect about the geometric image formation process I want to mention is the lens distortion.

249
00:30:53,000 --> 00:30:57,000
We haven't talked about lenses yet but we'll talk about lenses in the next unit

250
00:30:57,000 --> 00:31:04,000
and lenses are important because as already mentioned if we just use a very small pinhole

251
00:31:04,000 --> 00:31:10,000
we get very little light on the sensor plane so it's important to have a lens that collects light

252
00:31:10,000 --> 00:31:16,000
but if we have a lens system then often also there's a geometric distortion introduced

253
00:31:16,000 --> 00:31:22,000
and this linear projection model that we've seen before doesn't hold exactly anymore.

254
00:31:22,000 --> 00:31:33,000
There's lines in 3D that are projected onto curves in the image plane and this is called distortion.

255
00:31:33,000 --> 00:31:38,000
So the assumption of the linear projection that straight lines remain straight on the image plane

256
00:31:38,000 --> 00:31:42,000
is violated in practice due to these properties of the camera lenses

257
00:31:42,000 --> 00:31:44,000
and there's two types of distortions.

258
00:31:44,000 --> 00:31:48,000
There's radial distortion and tangential distortion

259
00:31:48,000 --> 00:31:56,000
and both of them luckily can be modeled for most camera models and most lens models relatively easily.

260
00:31:56,000 --> 00:32:01,000
So here's the formula that is most often used.

261
00:32:01,000 --> 00:32:06,000
What is done here is we take the point in camera coordinates

262
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:11,000
and first do the normalization by the C coordinate

263
00:32:11,000 --> 00:32:14,000
but we don't multiply the focal length yet

264
00:32:14,000 --> 00:32:20,000
and then we compute the radius and then with these components x, y and r

265
00:32:20,000 --> 00:32:31,000
we can multiply those with these or we can apply these polynomials to them

266
00:32:31,000 --> 00:32:34,000
that model the radial distortion and the tangential distortion.

267
00:32:34,000 --> 00:32:40,000
So we see here how the x, y point is transformed through this equation non-linearly

268
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:46,000
to yield a point x' that is the distorted point.

269
00:32:46,000 --> 00:32:49,000
So from the undistorted point we get a distorted point

270
00:32:49,000 --> 00:32:52,000
and then this distorted point is multiplied with the focal length

271
00:32:52,000 --> 00:32:58,000
so this is now the focal length from the pinhole model and the principal point is added

272
00:32:58,000 --> 00:33:06,000
and so this is everything that we need to do in order to also model very basic radial and tangential distortion.

273
00:33:06,000 --> 00:33:11,000
And the nice thing about this is that while this looks ugly

274
00:33:11,000 --> 00:33:15,000
at first glance the nice thing is that this can actually be undone.

275
00:33:15,000 --> 00:33:20,000
This effect is invertible because these transformations are typically monotonic

276
00:33:20,000 --> 00:33:25,000
so we can pre-compute undistorted images from distorted images

277
00:33:25,000 --> 00:33:32,000
and still apply the pinhole model directly on this undistorted images that have been simply pre-computed

278
00:33:32,000 --> 00:33:38,000
before using the image or the projection models that we want to use, the simple pinhole projection model

279
00:33:38,000 --> 00:33:40,000
and that's what's typically used in practice.

280
00:33:40,000 --> 00:33:43,000
We are undistorting the images before using them.

281
00:33:43,000 --> 00:33:46,000
Now this is a relatively simple model.

282
00:33:46,000 --> 00:33:51,000
More complex distortion models must be used for more complex lenses

283
00:33:51,000 --> 00:33:57,000
particularly wide angle lenses that do not follow this simple polynomial model.

284
00:33:57,000 --> 00:34:02,000
And here's some examples of how this looks like.

285
00:34:02,000 --> 00:34:05,000
You are probably familiar with this.

286
00:34:05,000 --> 00:34:11,000
There's an example of a checkerboard here and if we have so-called barrel distortion

287
00:34:11,000 --> 00:34:14,000
we obtain an effect like here.

288
00:34:14,000 --> 00:34:19,000
If we have the inverse of that, the negative effect is called pincushion distortion

289
00:34:19,000 --> 00:34:21,000
and we get a projection that looks like this

290
00:34:21,000 --> 00:34:27,000
and you can see in both cases the lines are not projected to lines but to curves.

291
00:34:27,000 --> 00:34:34,000
And also depending on the type of distortion the projected image becomes smaller or larger

292
00:34:34,000 --> 00:34:42,000
so you need to use a different crop on the image sensor to fully exploit the projected image.

